<http://data.silknow.org/object/4838f9e8-17b9-32f1-9d10-969e32de827d>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Linen embroidered with coloured silk, silver and silver-gilt thread"@en , "The front and side back of a woman's waistcoat, 1620-40, linen embroidered with silk and metal threads in a pattern of strawberries."@en , "This panel was probably intended to form part of a woman’s jacket. The triangular piece of fabric, called a  gore, inserted into the lower edge, suggests it served as one of the jacket fronts. It is embroidered in a repeating pattern of strawberries in shades of pink and red. The silver-gilt thread is worked mainly in plaited braid stitch and the silks in detached button-hole stitch. Such vibrantly embellished jackets were a popular style in English women’s fashions of the late 16th and early 17th centuries."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/eb577cd0-b806-3078-9557-62031a7508d8> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/6fa64293-deb4-332f-82e8-65c15b74d122> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/7bff5c6d-5165-359f-bebb-a376dd9085f8> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/4e594f29-90df-359f-9800-c458c9e7dac0> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/feb1e95f-0ed8-30ab-b8d3-df390fb3b9e8> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/1a67e4b8-ec55-3a80-b57b-1550e0b53eee> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "The front and side back of a woman's waistcoat, 1620-40, linen embroidered with silk and metal threads in a pattern of strawberries."@en , "Linen embroidered with coloured silk, silver and silver-gilt thread"@en , "This panel was probably intended to form part of a woman’s jacket. The triangular piece of fabric, called a  gore, inserted into the lower edge, suggests it served as one of the jacket fronts. It is embroidered in a repeating pattern of strawberries in shades of pink and red. The silver-gilt thread is worked mainly in plaited braid stitch and the silks in detached button-hole stitch. Such vibrantly embellished jackets were a popular style in English women’s fashions of the late 16th and early 17th centuries."@en ;
        dc:identifier                  "T.259-1926" ;
